The basic rule is:

SO + Adjective / Adverb
SUCH + Noun

This is one of the easiest rules once you identify whether the word after it is an adjective/adverb or a noun.

1. SO + Adjective

Use so before an adjective.

He is so strong that he can do the work easily. ✅

Here:

strong = adjective
so modifies the adjective

Structure:

so + adjective

More examples:

She is so beautiful.

He is so intelligent.

The question is so difficult.

2. SO + Adverb

Use so before an adverb.

He spoke so clearly that everybody understood his point. ✅

Here:

clearly = adverb
so modifies clearly

Structure:

so + adverb

Examples:

He ran so quickly that nobody could catch him.

She sings so beautifully that everyone enjoys listening to her.

3. SUCH + Noun

Use such before a noun.

He is such an idiot that he doesn't know the capital of his country. ✅

Here:

idiot = noun
an idiot = noun phrase
such modifies the noun phrase

Structure:

such + noun


4. SUCH + A/AN + Singular Countable Noun

This is extremely important.

When the noun is singular and countable, use:

such + a/an + noun

Examples:

He is such a good boy. ✅

She is such an intelligent girl. ✅

It was such a difficult question. ✅

Notice:

❌ such good boy
✅ such a good boy

❌ such intelligent girl
✅ such an intelligent girl


5. SO + Adjective + A/AN + Noun

Here's a very important alternative structure.

You can say:

so + adjective + a/an + singular noun

For example:

He is so good a boy that everyone likes him. ✅

She is so intelligent a girl that everyone admires her. ✅

It was so difficult a question that nobody could answer it. ✅

Compare:

such a good boy ✅

and

so good a boy ✅

Both are possible, but their structures are different.

Remember:

SUCH:

such + a/an + adjective + noun

SO:

so + adjective + a/an + noun
